        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the purpose of the hot water bath (55°C) test in commercial aerosol manufacturing?",
          "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "The 100% hot water bath test at 55°C is a mandatory safety measure for commercial aerosol filling that serves two purposes: leak detection and pressure resistance verification. Every filled can is immersed in water at 55°C, and any can that leaks or deforms is immediately identified and rejected before leaving the line. The 55°C temperature corresponds to the maximum permitted internal pressure for UN-compliant aerosol containers under normal transport conditions, making this test a realistic stress condition for distribution safety. This test cannot be economically replicated in laboratory settings, which is one reason commercial manufacturing cannot be bypassed in aerosol scale-up." }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What crimp measurements must be validated during aerosol pilot scale trials?",
          "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "The two critical crimp measurements are crimp diameter and crimp depth (sometimes called crimp height). Crimp diameter — typically measured with a dedicated crimp gauge — must fall within the valve manufacturer's specified tolerance, usually ±0.1 mm, to ensure the gasket is compressed correctly. Crimp depth affects the sealing integrity of the gasket against the can curl. Both dimensions must be validated across the full run of a pilot batch using statistical sampling to confirm the line is producing consistently safe, leak-free crimps before any commercial quantity is committed." }
        },
