          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What are 'loss factors' for propellant in aerosol manufacturing and why are they important?",
          "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Propellant loss factor is the percentage of propellant that evaporates, bleeds off, or is lost to the atmosphere between the storage tank and the final sealed can during the gassing process. Loss factors vary depending on the filling method (under-the-cup vs through-the-valve), line speed, ambient temperature, and propellant volatility. Accurately quantifying loss factors during pilot scale trials is essential for cost planning in commercial production, since propellant often represents 15–30% of total product cost. Without pilot-scale data, commercial production cost projections will be inaccurate." }
        },
