<!-- PILOT SCALE – THE VERIFICATION STAGE -->
<section class="section" id="pilot-scale-the-verification-stage">
  <h2>Pilot Scale – The Verification Stage</h2>
  <p>Pilot scale is the engineering bridge between the lab bench and the commercial filling line. At this stage, the product is produced for the first time using semi-automatic or rotary indexing equipment capable of 50–200 cans per hour, exposing crimp and gassing behaviours that are invisible at the laboratory scale. The goal is to verify that the valve specifications developed during R&amp;D translate correctly to semi-industrial crimping conditions, that the gassing process — whether under-the-cup or through-the-valve — works efficiently, and that the formulation maintains its physical integrity under bulk fill conditions. The <a href="/how-contract-aerosol-filling-works/" target="_top">contract aerosol filling process guide</a> describes the equipment and process steps common to both pilot and commercial lines.</p>
  <p>Pilot trials generate the data required to set machine parameters for commercial lines. Because pilot filling operates at semi-industrial speeds, it exposes mechanical bottlenecks and formulation stability challenges — emulsion separation from nozzle shear, foam formation during concentrate filling, slow gassing rates from restrictive valve stems — that are not detectable in hand-filled R&amp;D samples. This stage also produces enough units for stability testing and consumer trials. The deliverables from pilot scale are crimp dimension data, propellant loss factor calculations, and validated fill-weight specifications ready for commercial line setup.</p>

